this look was built around them: \code{box_style = "modern"},
\code{orientation = "horizontal"}, \code{value_axis = "top"}, \code{width = 0.45},
\code{grid_colour = "white"}, \code{ticks = FALSE}, and \code{zeroline = FALSE}
(the zero reference is instead drawn as a thick white line under
every layer, see \code{zero_indicator} below, which reads better than a
black one against the light blue panel). Every one of them can
still be overridden like any other argument.

\code{orientation = "vertical"} is supported, mapped the same way, but
only \code{"horizontal"} (the default, and the only orientation the
published figures this look comes from actually use) has been
visually checked.
